Changes made to the original content (Apache License 2.0, section 4(b)):

  - Text was extracted from the HTML pages and reformatted into JSON/JS data.
  - Long descriptions and tables were split, normalized and, where noted in the
    build scripts, truncated; markup, navigation and scripts were removed.
  - Trademark names in human-readable prose (API descriptions, field
    descriptions, guide titles and guide text) were replaced at build time by
    tools/trademark.js with generic terms: "Red Hat OpenShift Container
    Platform", "OpenShift Container Platform", "Red Hat OpenShift" and
    "OpenShift" -> "platform" (articles adjusted); "Red Hat Enterprise Linux
    CoreOS" -> "host operating system"; "Red Hat Enterprise Linux" -> "host
    Linux distribution"; "Red Hat" -> "vendor". Technical identifiers are
    not changed (see "Identifiers kept for interoperability" below).
  - Search indexes, relationship data and a user interface were added. The
    user interface does not use the trademarks; it refers to the source as
    documentation published under Apache-2.0 and points to this file.
  - The "constraints and limitations" shown for each API are sentences
    extracted automatically (by pattern matching) from the original descriptions,
    plus differences computed between the 4.22, 4.21 and 4.20 editions. They are
    not authoritative; consult the original documentation.
  - The "general relationships" shown in the topology view are NOT part of the
    original documentation. They were compiled separately from general
    knowledge of Kubernetes-based platforms and are marked as such in the
    interface.
  - Usage examples (YAML, oc, REST) are templates generated from API names,
    group/versions and required fields; they are not taken from the original.

Identifiers kept for interoperability

Some strings that contain or resemble the marks are technical identifiers that
must match the real API to be useful, so they are kept unchanged: API group
names, kinds and paths (for example route.openshift.io, OpenShiftAPIServer),
namespace names (for example openshift-config), enum values and abbreviations
used as values (for example RHCOS, RHEL), and URLs (for example links to
docs.redhat.com). They are used only to identify the corresponding API
elements, not as branding of this project.

